WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.043
When you walk, your legs bend and straighten in turn.

2
00:00:06.983 --> 00:00:07.983
Who keeps the beat?

3
00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:13.367
In the early twentieth century, a common view held that walking is a chain of

4
00:00:13.367 --> 00:00:15.747
reflexes, like falling dominoes.

5
00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:20.287
Graham Brown tested this in cats, in 1911.

6
00:00:21.417 --> 00:00:24.657
He cut the spinal cord to remove input from the brain.

7
00:00:25.517 --> 00:00:29.497
He also cut the dorsal roots carrying sensory signals from the hind legs.

8
00:00:30.517 --> 00:00:33.677
If walking is a chain of reflexes, what should be left?

9
00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:41.267
Nothing rhythmic should remain.

10
00:00:42.527 --> 00:00:46.747
Instead, the ankle flexors and extensors still contracted in alternation.

11
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.560
The rhythm arose within the cord.

12
00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.800
A network that can do this is called a central pattern generator.

13
00:00:54.883 --> 00:00:58.423
Modern work often uses a newborn rodent's cord in a dish.

14
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:03.253
Drugs in the bath give a steady push, like holding down an accelerator.

15
00:01:04.193 --> 00:01:05.193
They carry no timing.

16
00:01:06.233 --> 00:01:09.853
Yet the flexor and extensor roots fire in alternating bursts.

17
00:01:10.713 --> 00:01:11.933
The beat comes from the cord.

18
00:01:12.933 --> 00:01:17.933
Graham Brown proposed two groups of neurons, one driving flexors and one driving

19
00:01:17.933 --> 00:01:18.933
extensors.

20
00:01:19.583 --> 00:01:21.283
Each group inhibits the other.

21
00:01:22.223 --> 00:01:26.383
Activity switches when the active group's inhibition fades, or the held-down group

22
00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:27.383
escapes.

23
00:01:28.967 --> 00:01:31.827
It is reciprocal inhibition laid out in time.

24
00:01:32.727 --> 00:01:36.187
Whether real rhythm comes from such a simple pair is still debated.

25
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.070
Sensory input still matters.

26
00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:44.670
In newborn mouse cords, ankle nerve pulses between flexor bursts could start a

27
00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:45.670
flexor burst early.

28
00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:51.643
The cord makes the rhythm, and the senses adjust its timing.

29
00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:55.533
Which spinal neurons make the rhythm is still open.

30
00:01:56.353 --> 00:01:58.453
So is how much of this holds for human walking.

31
00:01:59.517 --> 00:02:01.357
The cord can keep the beat by itself.

32
00:02:02.277 --> 00:02:06.117
That beat reaches muscle as current, pushing pools up and down in turn.
